    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into some premature longitudinal cracking on a new asphalt section we laid last summer here in Phoenix. Can you provide a technical summary of the primary causes for this type of pavement failure, specifically focusing on the interplay between thermal expansion, bitumen viscosity changes, and subgrade compaction issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in hot climates like Phoenix is often a complex interplay of factors, but the primary culprits usually involve thermal stress, binder degradation, and inadequate support. High summer temperatures, especially in a desert environment, cause the asphalt mix to expand and contract significantly, which is exacerbated by the inherent brittleness of the bitumen when it cools or ages. If the subgrade compaction is poor, it offers insufficient support, allowing the pavement to deform under these thermal and load stresses, leading to the cracking pattern you're observing. These issues together create a failure mechanism where the pavement structure can't handle the differential movements.",
      "source": "target",
