    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. Could you go a bit deeper into the bitumen side of things? I'm specifically wondering how the rapid temperature fluctuations in the Phoenix area affect the viscosity and oxidative aging of the binder over the first year. I want to know if that aging process is making the surface more prone to those cracks during the cooler desert nights.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The rapid temperature fluctuations in Phoenix are a perfect recipe for accelerated binder aging, which is where the real trouble starts. When the asphalt binder heats up during the day, it becomes more fluid, but the constant cycling—especially the sharp drop during cooler nights—causes repeated thermal stresses. This cycling accelerates oxidative aging, which is essentially the binder breaking down due to reaction with oxygen. This degradation makes the bitumen stiffer and more brittle over time, meaning it loses its viscoelastic properties and becomes much more susceptible to cracking, even under moderate stress.",
      "source": "target",
